NAME
    clipboard - the system clipboard, in a window

SYNOPSIS
    clipboard

DESCRIPTION
    The clipboard is one piece of text every program shares. Windowed
    programs put a selection there with Ctrl+C and read it back with
    Ctrl+V, a shell reaches it through the /dev/clipboard character
    device, and this window shows that same text and lets it be edited.

    The window opens on whatever is in the clipboard and loads it into
    an editor. Typing changes the buffer on screen; the copy button
    writes it back to the clipboard, reload throws the screen away and
    reads the clipboard again, and clear empties both. Closing the
    window with Esc copies an edit that has not been copied yet, so
    nothing typed is lost by accident.

    The title bar names the device the text lives in, and the status
    line says whether the buffer and the clipboard agree and how many
    bytes the buffer holds.

    From a shell the clipboard is reached by redirection, since it is a
    device like any other:

        cat /dev/clipboard              print the clipboard
        echo hello > /dev/clipboard     replace it with "hello"
        cat /dev/clipboard > file       save it to a file
        a-command > /dev/clipboard      copy a command's output

    Writing the device replaces its whole contents, so the redirection
    is a copy and not an append.

KEYS
    printable keys     type into the buffer
    Enter              start a new line
    Backspace          delete backwards, joining lines at the start
    Delete             delete forwards
    Arrows, Home, End  move the caret
    a click            put the caret where it landed
    Ctrl+C             copy the line the caret is on
    Ctrl+X             cut the line the caret is on
    Ctrl+V             paste the clipboard at the caret
    Ctrl+A             copy the whole buffer to the clipboard
    Esc                copy a pending edit and close the window

    The three buttons under the buffer copy it to the clipboard,
    reload it from the clipboard, and clear both.

FILES
    /dev/clipboard   the clipboard itself: writing replaces it and
                     reading walks the current text from start to end.
                     It lives in RAM and does not survive a reboot.

SEE ALSO
    sticky(1), vedit(1)
